    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. One concern we have is the potential for \"orphaned\" or uncatalogued items that don't fit the primary categories during the initial intake phase. If we use LCC, how does the system handle those outliers or those items that seem to fall into a state of total disarray compared to the rest of the collection?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Library of Congress Classification includes specific \"notes\" and application notes designed explicitly to guide librarians in handling items that don't fit neatly into the main hierarchy, encouraging them to assign a related class with specific suffixes rather than leaving them unclassified. Unlike DDC, which has fewer such guides, LCC's flexibility allows archivists to create small, specialized numeric blocks or apply descriptive metadata labels to document historical disarray without disrupting the overall structure. This approach ensures that even outlier manuscripts retain a consistent, searchable location while maintaining the coherence of the main collection, which is crucial for long-term archival integrity.",
      "source": "target",
